20:A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. We show that under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement free replacement makes the ban costless, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits new identities first and then keeps targeting the newest ones. In two registered simulation tests, the second locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash, we compare R3 at matched budget and sanctions with uniform audits (B3) and a verified-history comparator, C$^*$. Without honest identity turnover, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to B3 on every fresh market (the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six), and R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}); in an exploratory comparison without churn, the development runner-up, a second verified-history comparator, C-thr(\const{12}), shows no consistent direction against R3. Welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0}.
21:Exploratory follow-ups after the report locate R3's advantage in the attacker's identity replacement and show that it erodes when honest providers also replace their identities. R3's audits then move to honest newcomers: with the frozen searches, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{abs_ch_fa1} to \site{abs_ch_fa5} between churn rates of $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} per round, and at $p = \const{0.03}$ and \const{0.05} both contrasts are lower than without churn on every sampled market. At $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{abs_ch_q5} (\site{abs_ch_q5d} with the doubled searches). These follow-ups rest on six markets, fixed parameters, illustrative churn rates, one comparator under churn, and searches of which \site{abs_ch_inc} of \site{abs_ch_cells} are flagged incomplete; their market-level intervals mostly include zero. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, planned and timestamped before they were drawn and running C-thr(\const{12}) under churn as well, met \site{abs_rp_met} of its five expectations with its primary search winners (\site{abs_rp_fmet} with the frozen winners alone); its mean changes rest mainly on two of the six markets, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero.
31:\paragraph{Checking newcomers is an old idea.} Intensive verification until an identity has earned credibility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host's jobs until the host passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}; customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}. Checking can also depend on what a participant reports~\cite{zarkoob2020spot}; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3's persistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything else is held equal?
33:\paragraph{The rule.} For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}). R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first $L$ rounds, auditing all of them when the round's quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/\const{2} \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits, $s$ being its served jobs (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity's first offer.
35:\paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.
37:\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}), and a bound on the audits that age-ranked targeting gives a replacement identity (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}). (ii) Two registered tests. Without honest churn, the prescribed searches give R3 a lower measured attacker gain than uniform audits on every fresh market, and they classify R3 and a development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent within the pre-set materiality margin; R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other. (iii) Two exploratory follow-ups, specified after the report, that locate R3's advantage. It sits in the attacker's identity replacement: without replacement most of the gap to uniform audits disappears. Under honest churn, at the tested rates: R3 then spends its audits on honest newcomers while an attacker that keeps its identity is rarely audited, and its measured advantage over the comparator is unresolved at churn rates of \const{0.01} and \const{0.02} per round and negative on all six markets at \const{0.03} under both search versions, while its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. A prospective-exploratory replication, planned and timestamped before six new markets were drawn, meets all of its expectations with its primary search winners and all but one with the frozen winners alone: under a descriptive criterion, R3's position against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits worsens from no churn, but the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, the comparison with C$^*$ at the middle rate is mixed across the new markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. (iv) A record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the failed predictions, and the follow-ups' plans, to be released with the paper.
39:\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets and without honest churn, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC and under D2+H3 (Section~\ref{sec:results}). Q4: the registered B3--R3 gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags. Q2 and Q3 (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-results}): the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and the registered W1 search (efficiency \site{int_w1}) supplies no efficiency bound for randomised attacks. The two follow-ups are in Sections~\ref{sec:reset} and~\ref{sec:churn}; the end of Section~\ref{sec:churn} reports the prospective replication on new markets.
48:\paragraph{Verification-based probation.} Credibility-based spot-checking accepts results according to a worker's verified history and analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; follow-on work treats imperfect checks~\cite{watanabe2010generalized}. BOINC's adaptive replication keeps a consecutive-valid count per host and trusts a host above a threshold~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}. CrowdEval screens workers with gold tasks before balancing gold and peer evaluation~\cite{qiu2018crowdeval}. Customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}, and a field experiment found no overall compliance gain from close supervision of new firms~\cite{gangl2014supervision}. In a model with adverse selection and moral hazard, optimal long-term contracts screen and then put high types on probation with monitoring that declines with the track record~\cite{bhaskar2025screening}. Our comparator family adapts the credibility and BOINC rules to a fixed audit quota (Section~\ref{sec:rules}).
56:\paragraph{Evaluation method.} Simulation-based strategic analysis has an established method~\cite{wellman2006methods,wellman2025egta}, including bootstrap inference for empirical games~\cite{wiedenbeck2014bootstrap}. Weak or incomplete attacks overstate robustness, and adaptive evaluations must be tailored to each defence~\cite{carlini2019evaluating,tramer2020adaptive}. Preregistration separates generating a hypothesis from testing it on new observations~\cite{nosek2018preregistration}. Our staged registration applies these tools.
76:\paragraph{R3.} R3 keeps B3's allocation, payment, tolerance, and enforcement and changes only which jobs it audits and when. \emph{Pacing:} with the integer allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, R3 reserves $\min(JL, A)$ audits for the last $L$ rounds, in which it audits every served job while audits remain, and spends the rest evenly over the earlier rounds, carrying unspent quota forward. \emph{Selection:} it first audits the served jobs of identities younger than $L$ rounds, newest first; it then spends $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ of the $r$ remaining audits on the newest established identities and the rest uniformly at random.
78:\paragraph{The factorial (second test).} The eight factorial rules and the comparators share B3's allocation and payment, refund-and-ban enforcement, no bond, the allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, and the fixed pair $\tau = \const{0.16}$, $L = \const{10}$, which the registered tuning of B3 and of R3 had each chosen. They differ in three parts: a priority phase P (served jobs of identities younger than $L$ first, newest first), newest-first residual targeting G ($\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual audits to the newest identities), and pacing S (R3's terminal reserve and even early quota, against B3's spread schedule). The rules $F_{PGS}$ include $F_{000} = $ B3 and $F_{111} = $ R3. B2 keeps its posted prices and serves only as a reference at the headline market.
80:\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective: the minimum, over the headline market, a development market, and the attack families, of the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin. The efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency, and at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain (Table~\ref{tab:comparator-dev}). The two best were rechecked at full budget; the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$), and the runner-up is C-thr($K = \const{12}$). Both full-budget scores were decided by D2 at dev0 (\const{0.103} against \const{0.098}), while under D2+H3 the runner-up left the lower gain (\const{0.076} against \const{0.097} at the headline market, \const{0.018} against \const{0.038} at dev0). This is a two-stage screen: it selects the winner among the two finalists and does not rank all twelve configurations at full budget. C$^*$ is the winner of this screen under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.
97:R3 audits every served job of identities younger than $L$ rounds (age counted from the first offer) whenever the round's audit count covers them. Under overload, jobs are audited by age group, newest first, with $k$ the round's available audit count; an identity that offers but wins no jobs still ages; within the focal identity's group of $n$ jobs, of which $s$ are its own, the number audited is hypergeometric with $k' = \min\{n, \max(0, k - H)\}$ draws, $H$ being the fresh jobs of strictly newer identities.
100:After its fresh window, a replacement identity is newer than every initial identity; while it is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/2 \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits. In a fixed population in which honest providers do not replace, a provider that keeps replacing can stay the newest identity, so R3's targeting of replacements persists beyond the window; with honest churn the uniquely-newest condition may no longer hold, so the guarantee may not apply, and coverage can be zero if newer jobs exhaust the relevant quota (Section~\ref{sec:churn}).
104:Appendix~\ref{app:proofs} gives a conditional renewal bound on a replacing attacker's audit rate under R3 (Theorem~\ref{thm:renewal}); its assumptions are not established for the implemented process, and no reported result depends on it.
121:X7-SC & State-conditioned: fresh, established, and terminal phases from its own audit exposure and pass count; reset or wait out a ban; voluntary exit & \const{15} & second test, all seven markets \\
149:Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
150:Q4 & Per market, an intersection-union test of $g_{F_{000}} - g_{F_{111}} > \const{0.10}$ under D2+H3 and $F_{111}$'s gross efficiency $> \const{0.90}$; the market's $p$-value is the larger of the two calibrated component $p$-values; Holm over the six markets; reported as $k$ of six, with failing markets named and undecided markets marked ``generalisation not established''. \\
156:Q4 replicates the B3--R3 comparison and R3's efficiency condition, not the complete amended claim of the first test. The two questions have separate multiplicity families; no paper-wide five percent guarantee is claimed. ``Confirmatory'' means specified before the draw; coverage admission rests on development fixtures and does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Q4 is an internal replication, not an independent team's.
162:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. Two exploratory follow-ups after the report bear on this comparison. Continued searches of \site{r_rb_cells} registered cells, \site{r_rb_flagged} whose doubling check was flagged and \site{r_rb_unflagged} unflagged X7-SC cells with held-out shortfalls (up to three rounds each), all passed the incremental-improvement check within three rounds, which bounds neither global convergence nor the remaining search error; they included no churn cells; with their final winners substituted, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{r_rb_q1} under D2+H3 and B3 minus R3 is \site{r_rb_q4}, and the largest change of a market difference is \site{r_rb_max} (fresh3, where the difference becomes positive). The development runner-up comparator, C-thr(\const{12}), evaluated on the same six markets without churn, gives the attacker a pooled D2+H3 gain lower than against R3 (runner-up minus R3: \site{r_ra_est}, [\site{r_ra_lo}, \site{r_ra_hi}]; ``\site{r_ra_cat}'', a label from nominal percentile intervals with the registered margin, not the registered calibration and Holm procedure); R3 has the lower gain on \site{r_ra_pos} of six markets, and \site{r_ra_minm} (\site{r_ra_min}) contributes most of the frozen pooled difference. Table~\ref{tab:ph-runnerup} gives all four family and search versions: every market-level $t$ interval includes zero, R3 has the lower gain on four or five of six markets, and C$^*$ minus runner-up is positive in all four; \site{r_ru_flag} of the \site{r_ru_flag_n} D2+H3 runner-up doubling checks and \site{r_ru_flag_x} of \site{r_ru_flag_xn} under X7-SC are flagged incomplete. The registered direction holds for C$^*$, not for verified-history targeting in general. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a $t$ interval over the per-market differences is [\site{r_ml_lo}, \site{r_ml_hi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_pos} of six markets positive, and [\site{r_ml_xlo}, \site{r_ml_xhi}] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [\site{r_ml_dlo}, \site{r_ml_dhi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_dpos} positive. With each rule's strongest training-selected family the interval is [\site{r_env_lo}, \site{r_env_hi}], with \site{r_env_pos} of six markets positive. The direction is therefore fairly stable across these analyses, while the size is not: every upper end is near the margin, so the equivalence label is the most fragile part; beyond these six markets the sign is not established either, since the frozen interval contains zero and the searches share inputs; on these markets the registered direction tests reject zero for the prescribed searches, and another comparator would be a different comparison. The frozen D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the attacker's pooled mean gain against C$^*$ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of \site{r_rel}. The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC's search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in \site{r_x7_same} of the \site{r_x7_cells} Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C$^*$ and fresh4 against R3 differ. With the headline market, the same holds in \site{r_x7_all} of \site{r_x7_all_n} market--rule cells, which is why most of prediction Q3-a's differences are exactly zero. With each rule's strongest training-selected family instead (Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope}), the pooled difference is \site{r_env_pool}, and \site{r_env_f1} on fresh1, where X5-AG/v2 is C$^*$'s strongest family. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain than C$^*$ (Section~\ref{sec:rules}); a comparator selected on attacker gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1} (its lowest bin; \site{r_hon_cs_2} in the next), and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns less per round (relative to C$^*$, a fraction of \site{r_hon_rel}; the registered diagnostic stores bin means, so no interval is available) and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
180:\paragraph{Identity replacement and the B3--R3 gap (exploratory follow-up).} D2+H3 is D2 plus one switch $x$ that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves \site{rs_hl_b3} more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and \site{rs_hl_r3} more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_hl_g1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_hl_g0} under D2. To see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that budget cut~\const{3} had removed (B3, R3, and C$^*$ at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each with a doubling check), under a plan and expectations written before any fresh D2 result existed, but after the headline contrast and the six fresh-market D2+H3 outcomes had been seen. This is a descriptive follow-up, not an independent replication, and it changes no registered decision. Pooled over the six markets with equal weights, the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_g1} (\const{95}\% paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{rs_g1_lo}, \site{rs_g1_hi}]) under D2+H3 and \site{rs_g0} ([\site{rs_g0_lo}, \site{rs_g0_hi}]) under D2 (doubled searches: \site{rs_g1_d} and \site{rs_g0_d}). The share of the gap that disappears without replacement, $S = \const{1} - G(\mathrm{D2})/G(\mathrm{D2{+}H3})$, is \site{rs_s} (doubled searches: \site{rs_s_d}). It exceeds one because the pooled D2 gap is slightly negative. Its paired-seed interval excludes zero conditional on these markets and selected policies, whereas the exploratory $t$ interval across the six market means, [\site{rs_gd0_t_lo}, \site{rs_gd0_t_hi}], includes zero; the gap is positive on \site{rs_gd0_pos} of the six markets (\site{rs_gd0_pos_d} with the doubled searches), and most of its negative value comes from fresh5 (without fresh5, $S$ is \site{rs_s_loo5}). We therefore do not establish a negative average gap across the market neighbourhood. The seed intervals above hold the six markets fixed; leaving out one market at a time gives $S$ from \site{rs_loo_lo} to \site{rs_loo_hi} over both search versions, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset} lists the gaps per market. The same holds for the Q1 contrast: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 gain difference is \site{rs_cr1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_cr0} under D2 ($t$ interval [\site{rs_cr0_lo}, \site{rs_cr0_hi}]; doubled searches [\site{rs_cr0_dlo}, \site{rs_cr0_dhi}]). The difference between the two prescribed searches, $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$, is \site{rs_i_b3} against B3, \site{rs_i_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_i_c} against C$^*$; it does not identify the optimal value of replacement. The expectations set in advance score as follows. $I(\mathrm{B3}) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_b}'' (\site{rs_sign_b} markets with the frozen searches, \site{rs_sign_bd} with the doubled ones). $I(\mathrm{C}^*) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_c}'' (\site{rs_sign_c} and \site{rs_sign_cd} markets; fresh3 gives \site{rs_f3_c} with the frozen searches). Pooled $S > \const{0.5}$: ``\site{rs_exp_s}''. Within the D2 family, at the fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the attacker cannot replace its identity. Appendix~\ref{sec:app-results} gives the knob-level checks, the sensitivity to references that replace identities, and the search flags of this follow-up; we keep the frozen searches as primary.
190:In the registered markets every honest provider keeps one identity for the whole episode, so after the start the newest identities are mostly the attacker's. This section asks what remains of R3's advantage when honest providers also replace identities. It is an exploratory follow-up after the report and changes no registered decision. The last part of the section reports a replication on new markets that was planned and timestamped before they were drawn. In both the follow-up and the replication, seed-bootstrap intervals condition on the six markets, market-level $t$ intervals use variation across those markets, and both condition on the selected search outputs and omit search uncertainty.
196:\paragraph{How firm the reversals are.} The intervals in Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn} hold the six markets fixed. Treating the markets as a sample, Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-t} gives exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences for every rate and search version: every one includes zero except B3 minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ with the doubled searches. The churn reversals therefore have narrow seed-bootstrap intervals on these six markets, intervals that hold the selected policies fixed and omit search uncertainty, and they are not established beyond these markets. Market by market: at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_5_neg} of the six markets and B3 minus R3 on \site{ch_q_5_neg} with the frozen searches, and on \site{ch_d_5_dneg} and \site{ch_q_5_dneg} with the doubled searches (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{ch_d_5_d}; B3 minus R3: \site{ch_q_5_d}); the frozen B3 value at this rate depends on one flagged cell. At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_3_neg} of six markets with the frozen searches and on \site{ch_d_3_dneg} with the doubled ones, while B3 minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_q_3_neg} and \site{ch_q_3_dneg}, so its positive pooled value there does not hold market by market. At $p = \const{0.02}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{ch_d_2_neg} markets: one market dominates the pooled value, and with the frozen searches it sets the sign. At $p = \const{0.01}$ the C$^*$ minus R3 difference is \site{ch_d_1_x} with the frozen searches and \site{ch_d_1_d} with the doubled ones, so the comparison with C$^*$ is not resolved at \const{0.01} or \const{0.02}, while the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_1_x} and \site{ch_q_1_d}. \site{ch_inc} of the \site{ch_inc_n} doubling checks at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} and \site{ch_inc_mid} of the \site{ch_inc_mid_n} at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} are flagged incomplete; the largest flagged change is \site{ch_flag_max}. Reselection within each rule's frozen reference menu under churn gives a nonzero training-set reference-score change in \site{ch_resel} of the \site{ch_resel_n} cells, with a largest absolute change of about \site{ch_resel_max}; this diagnostic does not bound the change in held-out gain, and the menus themselves were not regenerated.
200:\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it moves from \site{ch_fa_c1} to \site{ch_fa_c5} and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. With the doubled searches R3's value at $p = \const{0.01}$ is \site{ch_fa_1d} (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}). At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. With the frozen searches, on two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (with the doubled searches, none at $p = \const{0.01}$; Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and, once past its own priority window, is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. Where the selected attack does replace (\site{ch_rep_n} markets at $p = \const{0.05}$), R3 audits between \site{ch_rep_lo} and \site{ch_rep_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_rep_lo_d} and \site{ch_rep_hi_d} with the doubled ones, and the attacker is uniquely the newest job-holding identity (by first offer, ties excluded) in only \site{ch_new_lo} to \site{ch_new_hi} of the rounds in which it holds jobs (frozen); where it stays old (the same \site{ch_stay_n} markets under both search versions), R3 audits between \site{ch_stay_lo} and \site{ch_stay_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_stay_lo_d} and \site{ch_stay_hi_d} with the doubled ones. The measured share falls at each of the four sampled rates; four rates cannot show whether the decline is smooth or has a threshold. Audit share alone does not determine gain: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through \const{0.03}. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).
206:\paragraph{Prospective replication on new markets.} After two external reviews of this section, we planned a replication before any of its outcomes existed: the plan, its five expectations, the analysis code, and its tests were committed, timestamped with OpenTimestamps, and published, and six new markets (rep0 to rep5) were then drawn from a later Bitcoin block by the second test's market generator. The replication runs R3, C$^*$, B3, and the runner-up comparator C-thr(\const{12}) at $p = \const{0}$, \const{0.03}, and \const{0.05}, with D2+H3 attacks searched on the new markets, each rule's selected no-churn reference candidate replayed under the same churn, the registered evaluation and doubling check in every cell, and continued search of up to three rounds, under the same churn, for every cell whose doubling check was flagged. Of the \const{72} cells, \site{rp_cont} were flagged and continued (\site{rp_cont0} at $p = \const{0}$, \site{rp_cont3} at \const{0.03}, and \site{rp_cont5} at \const{0.05}; \site{rp_cont_r3} of them R3's), and every continued cell passed the continuation check within three rounds; passing that check is not a claim of convergence. The primary analysis uses each cell's frozen winner unless its doubling check was flagged, and the continued winner otherwise; the all-frozen set is a sensitivity analysis. The support criterion for a contrast at a rate is descriptive: its mean paired change from the same market's no-churn value is below zero, and the change is negative on at least five of the six markets; it is not a significance test. Its material companion requires a mean change at or below the registered Q1 margin of $-\const{0.05}$; both are reported for both winner sets. The fourth expectation carried a stated prospective probability of \const{0.5}. For the fifth, R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs is the number of the attacker's audited jobs over its served jobs, each summed over a market's \const{200} evaluation seeds; a market where the attacker serves no job at either rate counts as not lower. Seed-bootstrap intervals are \const{95}\% paired percentile intervals from \const{9999} resamples (seed \const{0}), resampling seeds within each market; market $t$ intervals use \const{5} degrees of freedom. We call the replication prospective-exploratory: its plan was fixed and stamped before its markets existed, but it is not part of either registered test and changes no registered decision.
208:\paragraph{Replication results.} Table~\ref{tab:replication} gives the paired changes and the absolute contrasts; Appendix~\ref{sec:app-rep} gives them per market, with the attack and reference terms, the efficiency of every rule, and R3's audit allocation. With the primary winners all five expectations are met: the support criterion holds for C$^*$ minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ (``\site{rp_e1}''), for B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} (``\site{rp_e2}''), for both at \const{0.03} (``\site{rp_e3}''), and for the runner-up minus R3 at \const{0.05} (``\site{rp_e4}''), and R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs is lower at \const{0.05} than without churn on at least five of six markets (``\site{rp_e5}''). The mean paired changes are \site{rp_d5} ($d$), \site{rp_q5} ($q$), and \site{rp_u5} ($u$) at $p = \const{0.05}$, and \site{rp_d3}, \site{rp_q3}, and \site{rp_u3} at \const{0.03}. The material companion holds for \site{rp_mat} of the six contrast and rate pairs with the primary winners and for \site{rpf_mat} with the all-frozen winners; the support criterion holds for \site{rp_sup} and \site{rpf_sup}. These means rest mainly on two markets: at $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, $d$ changes by \site{rp_d5_t0} and \site{rp_d5_t1} on rep0 and rep1, and by between \site{rp_d5_rlo} and \site{rp_d5_rhi} on the other four; with the primary winners the other contrasts and the rate \const{0.03} show the same pattern (Table~\ref{tab:rep-markets}). The material companion therefore describes the mean change, not a typical market. With the primary winners, the only market $t$ interval of a mean change that excludes zero is that of B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} ([\site{rp_q5_lo}, \site{rp_q5_hi}]); with the all-frozen winners the three intervals at \const{0.05} exclude zero and none at \const{0.03} does. With the all-frozen winners, the first, second, fourth, and fifth expectations are met (``\site{rpf_e1}'', ``\site{rpf_e2}'', ``\site{rpf_e4}'', ``\site{rpf_e5}''), and the third is ``\site{rpf_e3}'': C$^*$ minus R3 at \const{0.03} is lower than without churn on only \site{rpf_d3_k} of six markets, so it fails the support criterion while meeting the material companion. Continued search moved both the no-churn baselines and the churn cells: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 difference with the primary winners minus that with the all-frozen winners is \site{rp_sd0} without churn, \site{rp_sd3} at $p = \const{0.03}$, and \site{rp_sd5} at \const{0.05}. With the all-frozen winners the mean paired changes at \const{0.05} are \site{rpf_d5} ($d$), \site{rpf_q5} ($q$), and \site{rpf_u5} ($u$), further from zero than with the primary winners: at this rate the better-searched winner set gives the weaker changes. The paired changes therefore carry the search uncertainty of the baselines as well as that of the churn cells. As planned, the failed expectation is reported without a causal reading.
210:\paragraph{Ordering and audits on the new markets.} At $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad5} and negative on \site{rp_ad5_n} of six markets, the runner-up minus R3 is \site{rp_au5} (negative on \site{rp_au5_n}), and B3 minus R3 is \site{rp_aq5}, negative on \site{rp_aq5_n} markets (\site{rpf_aq5_n} with the all-frozen winners). The sign reversals of the earlier follow-up thus recur on the new markets in point estimates, but with the primary winners all three market $t$ intervals of these absolute contrasts include zero (Table~\ref{tab:replication}); the pooled seed-bootstrap interval of B3 minus R3, which holds the six markets fixed, is [\site{rp_aq5_blo}, \site{rp_aq5_bhi}] (Table~\ref{tab:rep-absolute}). At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad3} ([\site{rp_ad3_lo}, \site{rp_ad3_hi}]) and negative on only \site{rp_ad3_n} of six markets (\site{rpf_ad3_n} with the all-frozen winners), against all six under both search versions in the earlier follow-up; the new markets do not repeat that result. R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs falls from between \site{rp_fa0_lo} and \site{rp_fa0_hi} without churn to between \site{rp_fa5_lo} and \site{rp_fa5_hi} at $p = \const{0.05}$, and is lower on \site{rp_fa_below} of six markets. At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{rp_yj5} of the jobs and receive \site{rp_ya5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{rp_est5} (equal-weight means over the six markets; Table~\ref{tab:rep-alloc}). At $p = \const{0.03}$ the primary allocation covers only \site{rp_al3_n} of six markets, because the plan ran diagnostics for continued R3 winners only at $p = \const{0}$ and \const{0.05}. Under its selected attack at \const{0.05}, R3's mean gross efficiency is \site{rp_eff5} (lowest market \site{rp_eff5_min}) and C$^*$'s is \site{rp_effc5} (Table~\ref{tab:rep-terms}).
212:\paragraph{Scope.} The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is not specific to C$^*$. Every claim is restricted to the generator's market neighbourhood, the four rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the seed-bootstrap nor the market $t$ intervals cover search uncertainty. Within this model and at the tested rates, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ does not extend past the two lowest churn rates of the earlier follow-up, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}. On the new markets the pooled point estimates agree, but at \const{0.03} the comparison with C$^*$ is mixed across markets.
233:\caption{Exploratory follow-up: per churn rate and rule, under the rule's selected attack, the share of the attacker's jobs audited, the share of jobs served by honest identities younger than ten rounds and the share of all audits they receive, the audited share of established honest jobs, the number of markets whose selected attack never replaces its identity, and gross efficiency; means over the six markets; frozen / doubled searches where two values are given. These statistics were defined after the results at $p$ = \const{0.01} and \const{0.05} and before the runs at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03}.}\label{tab:ph-churn-audit}
236:\adjustbox{max width=\linewidth}{\input{tables/replication.tex}}
237:\caption{Prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets (rep0 to rep5), planned and timestamped before they were drawn: the change of $d$ = C$^*$ minus R3, $q$ = B3 minus R3, and $u$ = C-thr(\const{12}) minus R3 from the same market's no-churn value, as the mean over the six markets with its market $t$ interval (\const{5} degrees of freedom) and the number of markets where the contrast is lower than without churn; and the absolute contrasts at each churn rate $p$. Primary winners (the continued winner where the doubling check was flagged) and all-frozen winners. The intervals omit search uncertainty; per-market values and seed-bootstrap intervals are in Appendix~\ref{sec:app-rep}.}\label{tab:replication}
246:\paragraph{What they do not support.} The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empirical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished, and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C$^*$ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.
248:\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3's registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities. With honest churn the pooled advantage over C$^*$ is unresolved at the two lowest rates and negative from \const{0.03}, and the pooled advantage over B3 changes sign at the highest (Section~\ref{sec:churn}); that follow-up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and leaves the comparison with C$^*$ unresolved at the lowest rate. Its prospective replication on new markets adds the runner-up comparator but tests only two nonzero churn rates beside the no-churn baseline and a descriptive criterion, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
252:For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. Against R3 and C$^*$, the registered W1 searches selected policies with zero estimated gain and gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}; exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C$^*$ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level $t$ intervals. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and its measured advantage reverses: at $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{c_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{c_ch_q5} (\site{c_ch_q5d} with the doubled searches; the frozen value includes one search-flagged cell with a large improvement under doubling). Against C$^*$, the one verified-history comparator run under churn in that follow-up, the comparison is unresolved at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, and at \const{0.03} C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on every market under both search versions; against uniform audits its pooled advantage lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, fixed and timestamped before they were drawn, finds with its primary winners the same deterioration from no churn against C$^*$, the runner-up comparator, and uniform audits under its descriptive criterion; at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are negative on \site{c_rp_dn} and \site{c_rp_qn} of the new markets, but at \const{0.03} C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{c_rp_d3n}, the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. The registered Q1 and Q4 inferences use calibration admitted on development fixtures; the follow-up intervals are exploratory: seed-bootstrap intervals condition on the six sampled markets, while market-level $t$ intervals treat those markets as a sample; both hold the selected attacks and references fixed and omit search uncertainty, and the doubled searches are sensitivity analyses. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
278:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). R3's selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C$^*$ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the prescribed W1 search did not consider episode-level mixtures that reach lower empirical efficiency; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
324:\paragraph{Predictions.} Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} score every registered prediction from the report. For Q4-b, the original scoring script compared the report's raw component $p$-values, although the prediction is registered on calibrated values; the corrected script compares the calibrated values, \site{r_pred_q4b_g} against \site{r_pred_q4b_e} on each of fresh0, fresh2, and fresh5. The prediction remains fulfilled and the total is unchanged: a scoring correction with no change of verdict, separate from the corrected labels of Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4}. The original numerical scoring path, preserved in the released scoring output, retains estimates from completed searches even when their doubling checks were marked incomplete; it counts \site{r_pred_n_ful} predictions fulfilled and \site{r_pred_n_fail} failed. A separate eligibility analysis applies the two filters below. The registered scoring rule also says that an incomplete target, in the sense of the design's search-quality checks, is not assessed; those checks call a search-flagged cell incomplete while keeping its estimate for the prescribed search procedure; the rule does not say whether a prediction computed from a flagged search is itself incomplete. Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} adds two post hoc eligibility readings. Starting from the listed inputs of each scored comparison, we propagate flags through recorded upstream links, including references, attack banks, and warm starts. An inherited flag identifies a potentially relevant ancestor; it does not establish that the flagged candidate was selected or that the statistic would change. The map is released with the scorer, and neither sensitivity is designated as the official retrospective score. If a flagged listed input or recorded ancestor makes a Q3 policy comparison ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, Q3-d, and Q3-e are not assessed: \site{r_pred_q_ful} predictions are fulfilled, \site{r_pred_q_fail} fail, and \site{r_pred_q_na} are not assessed (counting direct inputs only, Q3-e stays fulfilled: \site{r_pred_qd_ful}, \site{r_pred_qd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_qd_na}). The broader sensitivity excludes flagged scored cells outside six decision-related predictions retained by convention: Q1-a, its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b (the design keeps the decisions but does not say how predictions about them are scored). A prediction still fails if any eligible cell fails; otherwise, an excluded required cell leaves it not assessed. Q1-c and Q2-a to Q2-d are then also not assessed: \site{r_pred_d_ful} fulfilled, \site{r_pred_d_fail} failed, and \site{r_pred_d_na} not assessed (counting direct inputs only: \site{r_pred_dd_ful}, \site{r_pred_dd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_dd_na}). No reading turns a failed prediction into a fulfilled one or changes a registered decision; the marked rows of Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} are those a stricter reading changes, and their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed searches. The predictions differ in stakes and are not independent (Q1-a, its alternative, and Q1-d are decided by the same Q1 categories, and Q3-e passes by less than one hundred-thousandth), so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3's and C$^*$'s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held (the first is not assessed under the broader reading).
