Replication results. Table 10 gives the paired changes and the absolute contrasts; Appendix B
gives them per market, with the attack and reference terms, the efficiency of every rule, and R3’s
audit allocation. With the primary winners all five expectations are met: the support criterion holds
for C∗ minus R3 at p = 0.05 (“met”), for B3 minus R3 at 0.05 (“met”), for both at 0.03 (“met”),
and for the runner-up minus R3 at 0.05 (“met”), and R3’s audited share of the attacker’s jobs is
lower at 0.05 than without churn on at least five of six markets (“met”). The mean paired changes
are −0.146 (d), −0.142 (q), and −0.118 (u) at p = 0.05, and −0.063, −0.065, and −0.051 at 0.03.
The material companion holds for 6 of the six contrast and rate pairs with the primary winners
and for 6 with the all-frozen winners; the support criterion holds for 6 and 5. These means rest
mainly on two markets: at p = 0.05 with the primary winners, d changes by −0.352 and −0.344
on rep0 and rep1, and by between −0.072 and −0.015 on the other four; with the primary winners
the other contrasts and the rate 0.03 show the same pattern (Table 30). The material companion
therefore describes the mean change, not a typical market. With the primary winners, the only
market t interval of a mean change that excludes zero is that of B3 minus R3 at 0.05 ([−0.282,
−0.003]); with the all-frozen winners the three intervals at 0.05 exclude zero and none at 0.03 does.
With the all-frozen winners, the first, second, fourth, and fifth expectations are met (“met”, “met”,
“met”, “met”), and the third is “not met”: C∗ minus R3 at 0.03 is lower than without churn on only
4 of six markets, so it fails the support criterion while meeting the material companion. Continued
search moved both the no-churn baselines and the churn cells: the pooled C∗ minus R3 difference
with the primary winners minus that with the all-frozen winners is −0.023 without churn, −0.034
at p = 0.03, and 0.046 at 0.05. With the all-frozen winners the mean paired changes at 0.05 are
−0.214 (d), −0.175 (q), and −0.156 (u), further from zero than with the primary winners: at this
rate the better-searched winner set gives the weaker changes. The paired changes therefore carry
the search uncertainty of the baselines as well as that of the churn cells. As planned, the failed
expectation is reported without a causal reading.

